WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.292 --> 00:00:06.836
I have a death wish.

00:00:07.921 --> 00:00:12.926
Now before you just boo me off the stage
for saying such a thing in a pandemic,

00:00:12.926 --> 00:00:15.136
I'll explain with a little back story,

00:00:15.136 --> 00:00:18.056
starting with my second grade gym class.

00:00:18.098 --> 00:00:20.600
This was the longest,

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:23.395
most humiliating hour of the week.

00:00:23.436 --> 00:00:27.816
My PE teacher, Mr. Jensen,
was a former drill sergeant,

00:00:27.857 --> 00:00:32.070
and I always felt like the weakest kid.

00:00:32.112 --> 00:00:33.238
On my report card,

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:37.242
he checked the boxes corresponding
to what must have been important

00:00:37.284 --> 00:00:39.911
for kids' physical
development in the '70s.

00:00:40.328 --> 00:00:42.789
Oh, except the one
on leadership qualities,

00:00:42.831 --> 00:00:44.874
he left that one unchecked.

00:00:45.542 --> 00:00:47.168
Then he added a note.

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:51.047
He said, "Andrea
has difficulty kicking balls."

00:00:51.047 --> 00:00:54.884
(Laughter)

00:00:54.926 --> 00:00:59.347
My dad, who was always the one to see
people's gifts instead of their limits,

00:00:59.389 --> 00:01:01.683
wrote a letter back to Mr. Jensen.

00:01:01.725 --> 00:01:04.728
He said, "Andrea may have
difficulty kicking balls,

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:08.231
but you should see her stand
on her head and do cartwheels."

00:01:08.231 --> 00:01:09.566
(Laughter)

00:01:10.191 --> 00:01:13.987
As a kid, I was usually
the smallest on the playground,

00:01:13.987 --> 00:01:15.614
never athletic,

00:01:15.655 --> 00:01:19.326
picked last for, you guessed it, kickball.

00:01:19.326 --> 00:01:22.454
I took solace in my dad's sincere support.

00:01:22.871 --> 00:01:25.165
But now, as a business owner,

00:01:25.206 --> 00:01:28.627
I can see that ball kicking
can really come in handy

00:01:28.668 --> 00:01:30.545
(Laughter)

00:01:30.587 --> 00:01:34.466
Anyway, in that one simple sentence
to a short-sighted gym teacher

00:01:34.507 --> 00:01:36.301
"but you should see her,"

00:01:36.343 --> 00:01:40.805
my dad showed me that focusing
on strengths more than weaknesses

00:01:40.847 --> 00:01:42.307
feels really good.

00:01:42.891 --> 00:01:45.435
That there are other ways
to look at the world

00:01:45.477 --> 00:01:48.521
and that it's important
to recognize each other's gifts.

00:01:49.522 --> 00:01:51.399
So with all that great karma

00:01:51.441 --> 00:01:54.361
my dad had built up
around appreciation with me,

00:01:54.402 --> 00:01:58.281
it only seems right that some praise
would ultimately come his way.

00:01:58.865 --> 00:02:01.951
Long into his fruitful
and active retirement,

00:02:01.993 --> 00:02:04.621
he was featured in a hometown
newspaper story.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:06.873
The article described the many ways

00:02:06.873 --> 00:02:10.001
he contributed to our small
Midwestern town.

00:02:10.335 --> 00:02:14.005
And he sent a copy of that piece
to my siblings and me

00:02:14.047 --> 00:02:15.965
with a short handwritten note.

00:02:16.007 --> 00:02:17.634
He was always really modest,

00:02:17.676 --> 00:02:18.843
so he just said,

00:02:18.843 --> 00:02:23.098
"Well, it's better than having
a eulogy read over a casket."

00:02:26.059 --> 00:02:29.270
My dad died seven years
ago at the age of 96,

00:02:29.312 --> 00:02:34.067
and he was surrounded by family
and friends and two hospice workers.

00:02:34.776 --> 00:02:40.115
He left this world not 20 feet
from where he’d come into it.

00:02:40.448 --> 00:02:43.993
He was born and he died
in the same house.

00:02:44.661 --> 00:02:48.248
I like to think he died
as well as he'd lived.

00:02:48.289 --> 00:02:50.208
On his own terms.

00:02:51.334 --> 00:02:53.628
I had the honor of giving the eulogy,

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:57.841
and ultimately he'd chosen
cremation over that "casket."

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:01.344
As I looked over at my dad's ashes,

00:03:01.386 --> 00:03:05.724
I had to smile because, you know,
our dad really loved beer.

00:03:06.057 --> 00:03:09.644
So instead of putting
his ashes in a blasé urn,

00:03:09.644 --> 00:03:12.814
we put them in a big, shiny beer growler.

00:03:12.856 --> 00:03:13.982
(Laughter)

00:03:15.066 --> 00:03:18.987
So my remarks over the growler
were a sincere tribute

00:03:18.987 --> 00:03:20.822
to a superb human being.

00:03:21.406 --> 00:03:24.200
Not unusual as far as eulogies go,

00:03:24.242 --> 00:03:26.077
except for one thing.

00:03:26.119 --> 00:03:27.787
He'd already heard it.

00:03:28.705 --> 00:03:31.750
Those many years earlier,
after I'd received his note

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:35.754
about how having nice things
said about you while you're alive

00:03:35.795 --> 00:03:37.547
beats the alternative,

00:03:37.589 --> 00:03:39.215
I wrote my dad a letter.

00:03:40.049 --> 00:03:43.136
And the theme of the letter
reflected a common thread

00:03:43.136 --> 00:03:45.638
that I had noticed in his long life.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:47.348
The theme of building.

00:03:48.057 --> 00:03:51.519
Our dad had helped to build
so many things.

00:03:52.729 --> 00:03:56.316
Gun emplacements in World War II;

00:03:56.316 --> 00:03:59.110
a new industrial park;

00:03:59.152 --> 00:04:04.449
a vibrant hobby as a self-taught
and later acclaimed woodcarver;

00:04:04.491 --> 00:04:07.452
confidence in others;

00:04:07.494 --> 00:04:10.205
a many-decades-long marriage;

00:04:10.205 --> 00:04:12.707
a family, a home.

00:04:13.458 --> 00:04:17.879
And he had the chance to read it all
long before he died.

00:04:18.922 --> 00:04:21.216
So that had me asking,

00:04:21.216 --> 00:04:24.385
why are eulogies only for dead people?

00:04:26.179 --> 00:04:30.391
Why do we wait so long
to recognize each other's gifts?

00:04:31.684 --> 00:04:35.480
Why are the truest compliments
and the sincerest sentiments

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:37.482
said about people we love

00:04:37.482 --> 00:04:41.528
when they can't hear and savor
and relish them?

00:04:42.695 --> 00:04:47.492
And how do we honor all those around us
who are very much alive?

00:04:48.993 --> 00:04:52.789
So what if we turn regret on its head

00:04:52.789 --> 00:04:55.667
and take all that love
and conscientiousness

00:04:55.708 --> 00:04:59.254
that we habitually
express after people die

00:04:59.295 --> 00:05:01.506
and do it while they're still here?

00:05:02.423 --> 00:05:05.760
Because doing that eases the pain
of death and regret

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:08.137
for both the dying and the living.

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:14.602
So I set out on an intentional quest
to bear witness to people who are dying.

00:05:15.061 --> 00:05:16.855
As a hospice volunteer,

00:05:16.855 --> 00:05:19.107
I'm learning that those who are dying,

00:05:19.148 --> 00:05:21.109
they want to know that they're loved.

00:05:21.651 --> 00:05:23.319
That they've loved well.

00:05:24.237 --> 00:05:28.116
They feel regret for all sorts of things.

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:32.745
For things they didn't do
and words they didn't say.

00:05:33.621 --> 00:05:37.625
Deep down, they want to know
that their lives have mattered.

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:41.254
They feel really mortal.

00:05:41.921 --> 00:05:43.256
Because they are.

00:05:44.173 --> 00:05:45.341
As am I.

00:05:46.175 --> 00:05:47.343
As are you.

00:05:49.053 --> 00:05:51.848
So when we learn a loved one may be dying,

00:05:51.890 --> 00:05:54.017
we face a pivotal choice.

00:05:54.058 --> 00:05:56.102
We can choose to say nothing

00:05:56.144 --> 00:05:59.480
and hope that our words
will sufficiently honor the person

00:05:59.522 --> 00:06:01.900
who's no longer here with us.

00:06:02.859 --> 00:06:06.529
Or we can step up and express
our love and appreciation

00:06:06.571 --> 00:06:08.239
while they're still here.

00:06:09.073 --> 00:06:13.828
And we can honor all those around us
who are very much alive.

00:06:15.747 --> 00:06:19.626
I call this intentional honoring
of others “Gracenotes”.

00:06:19.667 --> 00:06:21.794
And whether they’re written or spoken,

00:06:21.836 --> 00:06:24.797
they're this means of freely
and openly acknowledging

00:06:24.839 --> 00:06:27.216
someone's presence and gifts.

00:06:28.301 --> 00:06:32.305
I know in my bones that these sentiments
lessen the pain of grief

00:06:32.347 --> 00:06:34.307
and increase its grace.

00:06:35.141 --> 00:06:37.644
Like a musical grace note,

00:06:37.644 --> 00:06:39.395
they're that extra embellishment

00:06:39.437 --> 00:06:42.273
that makes something
beautiful even better.

00:06:43.483 --> 00:06:48.071
With Gracenotes, we let our family,
our friends, our kids,

00:06:48.112 --> 00:06:51.741
even our colleagues know
not just that they matter

00:06:51.783 --> 00:06:53.368
but how they matter.

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:58.456
And these notes also help us
to overcome the illusion,

00:06:58.498 --> 00:07:03.127
the illusion that there'll always
be more time to let others know

00:07:03.169 --> 00:07:04.796
how they're impacting the world.

00:07:07.256 --> 00:07:11.177
So what do you think keeps us
from "gracing" each other this way?

00:07:12.095 --> 00:07:13.596
Lack of time?

00:07:14.514 --> 00:07:16.516
Fear of feeling awkward?

00:07:17.308 --> 00:07:18.935
Unresolved anger?

00:07:20.019 --> 00:07:21.854
Maybe a lack of forgiveness.

00:07:23.356 --> 00:07:26.234
With that in mind, I'll tell you
a bit about my friend Sandy.

00:07:27.443 --> 00:07:31.447
For much of her life, she had this
complicated relationship with her mom.

00:07:31.823 --> 00:07:35.076
She was holding on
to decades of resentment.

00:07:35.076 --> 00:07:38.204
Some of it was tied up
in her mom's alcoholism.

00:07:38.955 --> 00:07:41.124
But now her mom was dying of cancer.

00:07:41.124 --> 00:07:44.377
And as heart-wrenching as it was to admit,

00:07:44.419 --> 00:07:47.422
she said sometimes she wanted her to die.

00:07:49.173 --> 00:07:52.677
Sandy and I happened to go for a walk
not long after my dad passed,

00:07:52.677 --> 00:07:57.724
and I said I felt this sense of freedom
and a lack of regret,

00:07:57.765 --> 00:07:59.058
and I thought it had to be

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:01.477
because I gave him that note
before he died.

00:08:02.061 --> 00:08:04.480
So Sandy decided to write her mom a note,

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:07.066
and she included
an honoring set of memories

00:08:07.066 --> 00:08:09.360
about what she did love about her mom.

00:08:10.445 --> 00:08:12.155
She called me later, she said,

00:08:12.196 --> 00:08:13.406
"You know what?

00:08:13.990 --> 00:08:15.450
It was like magic.

00:08:16.492 --> 00:08:18.745
I started liking my mom.

00:08:19.871 --> 00:08:21.539
I forgave her.

00:08:22.457 --> 00:08:24.167
I felt compassion for her.

00:08:25.334 --> 00:08:26.919
My heart got softer."

00:08:30.214 --> 00:08:31.549
Here's the thing.

00:08:32.091 --> 00:08:33.843
When we're writing a Gracenote,

00:08:33.885 --> 00:08:37.764
you're not trying to get published
in a poetry anthology.

00:08:38.389 --> 00:08:40.933
You don't have to sound like Shakespeare.

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:45.438
My guess is you're probably not trying
to get a job at Hallmark.

00:08:46.439 --> 00:08:48.399
Just want to sound like you.

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:50.985
You just need to be willing to try.

00:08:52.236 --> 00:08:57.283
You're aiming for truth,
authenticity, love.

00:08:59.243 --> 00:09:03.372
What I've learned over and over
is that the pain of regret

00:09:03.414 --> 00:09:07.418
is always greater than the challenge
of writing a Gracenote.

00:09:09.337 --> 00:09:13.925
A moment of grace can be that simple
and that profound.

00:09:14.717 --> 00:09:18.179
It's an experience
that forever touches the dying

00:09:18.221 --> 00:09:20.223
and those who are left behind.

00:09:22.391 --> 00:09:23.935
So what now?

00:09:25.019 --> 00:09:27.396
You might be sitting there
thinking what you'd say

00:09:27.396 --> 00:09:28.898
or how to get started.

00:09:29.732 --> 00:09:32.443
Business groups and long-term
care groups that I speak to

00:09:32.443 --> 00:09:35.404
use what I like to call
the Mad Libs approach.

00:09:36.197 --> 00:09:38.741
Maybe you played Mad Libs
in middle school.

00:09:39.117 --> 00:09:41.035
Maybe you still play Mad Libs.

00:09:41.494 --> 00:09:44.080
It’s that fun, 
fill-in-the-blank word game.

00:09:44.664 --> 00:09:48.835
So with a little bit of Mad Libs style,
we get this easy-to-use

00:09:48.876 --> 00:09:50.962
Gracenotes road map.

00:09:51.963 --> 00:09:55.007
"You are the only person I know who ..."

00:09:57.343 --> 00:09:59.971
"I always laugh when I think about ..."

00:10:01.472 --> 00:10:03.724
"You will leave a legacy around ..."

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:08.688
Bottom line, you can't do this wrong.

00:10:08.729 --> 00:10:10.022
It's impossible.

00:10:10.481 --> 00:10:12.567
And if writing’s not your thing,

00:10:12.567 --> 00:10:14.527
what if you made a video?

00:10:15.027 --> 00:10:16.195
A drawing?

00:10:16.904 --> 00:10:19.532
Maybe you're really good
at PowerPoint decks.

00:10:20.616 --> 00:10:22.869
Could you write a grace sentence?

00:10:24.704 --> 00:10:27.290
The medium doesn't matter.

00:10:27.665 --> 00:10:29.417
What matters is the doing.

00:10:31.627 --> 00:10:34.672
What I've learned
in this journey of my own

00:10:34.714 --> 00:10:36.799
and in talking to others
about their losses,

00:10:36.799 --> 00:10:41.220
is that Gracenotes create
the sense of completeness,

00:10:41.262 --> 00:10:43.431
contentedness and calm.

00:10:45.308 --> 00:10:48.227
I vividly recall the last night
my dad was alive.

00:10:49.353 --> 00:10:52.857
By then he was unconscious,
and everyone else had gone to bed.

00:10:53.566 --> 00:10:57.528
I sat with him
with my hand softly on his.

00:10:58.279 --> 00:11:01.699
And on this unrepeatable night,

00:11:01.741 --> 00:11:05.203
I didn't have to worry
about trying to find the words

00:11:05.244 --> 00:11:07.079
to tell him all that he'd meant to me

00:11:07.121 --> 00:11:09.749
and only hope that he could
hear what I said.

00:11:10.499 --> 00:11:13.836
Instead, I could be present to his dying.

00:11:13.878 --> 00:11:17.632
I could affirm that if this
was his time to move on,

00:11:17.632 --> 00:11:18.925
that was OK.

00:11:20.134 --> 00:11:23.429
I could love him with presence
and with touch,

00:11:23.471 --> 00:11:27.725
knowing full well that my Gracenote
those many years earlier

00:11:27.767 --> 00:11:33.022
had been a sincere and thorough
rendering of his life well lived.

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:37.235
Ever since, I've felt very little regret.

00:11:37.276 --> 00:11:41.322
And I know it's because I shared
my full heart with him

00:11:41.364 --> 00:11:43.032
before it was too late.

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:49.205
So I hope, I deeply hope,

00:11:49.247 --> 00:11:52.250
that you don't know anyone
who's actively dying.

00:11:53.376 --> 00:11:57.088
But given the times we're in,
that may well not be the case.

00:11:58.089 --> 00:11:59.757
No matter what,

00:11:59.799 --> 00:12:02.802
I encourage you to just look around.

00:12:03.803 --> 00:12:09.058
People everywhere
are dying to be seen and heard,

00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:11.018
to know how they matter.

00:12:11.936 --> 00:12:14.063
They're dying to get your Gracenote.

00:12:15.273 --> 00:12:17.525
So that is my death wish.

00:12:18.526 --> 00:12:22.405
That you see how your note,
no matter what form it takes,

00:12:22.446 --> 00:12:26.617
is like an oasis in a desert of people

00:12:26.659 --> 00:12:30.329
who are thirsty to know
they're making a difference.

00:12:31.414 --> 00:12:32.999
Like my dad said,

00:12:32.999 --> 00:12:36.127
it's better than having
a eulogy read over a casket.

00:12:38.254 --> 00:12:39.463
Thank you.

00:12:39.505 --> 00:12:43.175
(Applause)

